    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:1",
      "id": "ex-distinguished-subset-and-localisation-primes",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L1]",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "[L1] says `D(x)` is the set of prime ideals that do not contain `x` and cites `[[lem-distinguished-subset-identities]]`. But that lemma's Statement is only `D(0)=∅`, `D(1)=Spec(R)`, `D(fg)=D(f)∩D(g)`, and `D(f^n)=D(f)`. The defining sentence `D(f)=\\{\\mathfrak p\\in\\operatorname{Spec}(R):f\\notin\\mathfrak p\\}` appears in `def-principal-distinguished-subset-of-spectrum`, not in the cited lemma, so the citation does not license [L1].",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "bc6938d50ac4042f900f76ae5e36ca1203e27608e9e6aa434510126ae0c02789"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:6:2",
      "id": "thm-noetherian-ring-has-finitely-many-minimal-primes",
      "location": "Facts & Assumptions [L1]",
      "defect": "citation-inaccurate",
      "evidence": "[L1] claims `The nilradical Nil(R)=sqrt((0)) is a radical ideal` and cites `[[def-nilradical-and-reduced-ring]]`. That definition says only `Nil(R)=sqrt((0))` and characterizes its elements as nilpotent; it does not state that `sqrt((0))` is radical. The needed radicality comes from idempotence of radicals, proved in `lem-radical-is-an-ideal` (`sqrt(sqrt(I))=sqrt(I)`). So the cited dependency is too weak for [L1] as written.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "6803972c8a5fccefb5c94a760c9305ca2d27d9a3d55b6bc82a87a13ceaa35794"
    }
  ],
